Spontaneous Pneumothorax
Respiratory System
Sudden unilateral pleuritic pain, Unilateral hyperresonance, Absent breath sounds, Visible pleural edge

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-045
Reason for Visit: Sudden, agonizing, sharp chest pain and acute breathlessness occurring at rest in a healthy young adult, requiring immediate assessment to rule out lung collapse.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a tall, thin young adult who experienced a sudden onset of severe, unilateral pleuritic chest pain and breathlessness while sitting quietly. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively diagnose a spontaneous pneumothorax and rule out life-threatening tension physiology before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I was just reading a book and suddenly it felt like my right lung popped, and now taking a breath is incredibly sharp and painful."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began abruptly 1 hour ago. The patient describes a sudden onset of sharp, unilateral pleuritic chest pain localized to the right chest. This was immediately followed by acute dyspnea occurring at rest. The patient is a tall, thin, young individual (classic Marfanoid habitus phenotype). There is no history of trauma, heavy lifting, or underlying lung disease. The patient is taking rapid, shallow breaths to minimize the pain.
Functional Status: Fully independent; currently highly distressed and refusing to move.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; risk of rapid progression to tension pneumothorax.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 115 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 26 breaths/min | SpO2: 94% on room air | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Tall, thin build. Alert but anxious, in obvious respiratory distress, splinting the right side of the chest.
Physical Exam: Chest examination reveals asymmetrical chest expansion with diminished movement on the right side. Percussion demonstrates striking hyperresonance on the affected right side compared to the left. Auscultation reveals markedly decreased or completely absent breath sounds unilaterally on the right. Decreased tactile fremitus is noted. The trachea is central (no obvious tension physiology yet). Tachycardia is present, proportional to the size of the pneumothorax and pain. No subcutaneous emphysema is palpated.
Environmental Observations: Patient is clutching their chest, visibly afraid to inhale deeply.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications taken.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Spontaneous Pneumothorax - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of unilateral hyperresonance and absent breath sounds (PE typically has clear, equal breath sounds), and the classic young, tall, thin phenotype strongly favors pneumothorax.

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is strictly pleuritic and unilateral, occurring in a very young patient without cardiac risk factors, and physical signs localize entirely to the lung pleura.

Pleurisy/Viral Pleuritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete absence of breath sounds and hyperresonance on the affected side (pleurisy maintains normal breath sounds but adds a friction rub).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the textbook, undeniable physical signs of a primary spontaneous pneumothorax: sudden unilateral pleuritic pain, unilateral hyperresonance, and absent breath sounds in a tall, thin, young adult. A subpleural bleb has ruptured, allowing air to escape into the pleural space and collapse the right lung. While there are currently no signs of tension (trachea is central, BP is stable), the marked tachypnea, tachycardia, and borderline hypoxia indicate a clinically significant collapse. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would carry an unacceptable risk of the air leak progressing to a fatal tension pneumothorax. An urgent after-hours home visit was required to physically confirm the diagnosis, rule out immediate tension, provide analgesia, and arrange immediate transfer for radiological confirmation and potential chest tube insertion.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral Ibuprofen 400mg and Paracetamol 1000mg to manage the acute pleuritic pain. Initiated low-flow oxygen via nasal prongs to assist with pleural air resorption. Activated EMS for urgent transfer to the Emergency Department for an erect chest X-ray.
Positive Behavioral Change: Breathe in slowly through your nose and out gently through pursed lips; actively controlling your breath in this manner prevents sudden coughing or gasping, which directly stops more air from being forced out of the hole in your lung into your chest cavity.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable; hospital assessment required.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover provided directly to the responding paramedics and receiving ED.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Patient is being transferred via ambulance. Do not attempt to stand up, bear down (Valsalva), or cough forcefully while awaiting transport.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical exam is classic for a collapsed lung, but an urgent chest X-ray is required at the hospital to visually measure exactly how much of the lung has collapsed.
Clinical Course: If the collapse is small, it may heal on its own; if it is large, a doctor will need to insert a small tube to physically suck the trapped air out and reinflate the lung.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Tell the paramedics immediately if you suddenly feel extremely dizzy, if your lips turn blue, or if you feel a tearing sensation in your neck.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A tiny, weak blister on the surface of your lung popped, allowing air to leak into your chest cavity and physically crush your lung; we must send you to the hospital to safely release this trapped air before the pressure builds up and affects your heart.
Actionable Steps: Do not hold your breath and bear down (like trying to forcefully pop your ears); creating this high pressure in your chest will directly force more air out of the blister, rapidly making the lung collapse worse.

